  data: {
    LLM: "If you have the Ace and King of Trumps, lead the Ace to draw trumps out, then lead the King to let your partner smear a 10 or Jack. If you are defending, try to save your Low (2) for a trick you can win, or smear it on your partner's trick if you think they are winning.\n\nThe 'Game' Count: Counting the 10s is vital. There are four 10s (40 points). Aces are 4 each (16). Kings are 3 each (12). Queens 2 (8). Jacks 1 (4). Total usually around 80 points. Winning the 'Game' point often decides the match.\n\nBidding against the Dealer: The dealer has the last say and can 'steal' the bid for the same amount (in some rules). Bid high enough to force them to 3 or 4.",
    Player: "If you have the Ace and King of Trumps, lead the Ace to draw trumps out, then lead the King to let your partner smear a 10 or Jack. If you are defending, try to save your Low (2) for a trick you can win, or smear it on your partner's trick if you think they are winning.\n\nThe 'Game' Count: Counting the 10s is vital. There are four 10s (40 points). Aces are 4 each (16). Kings are 3 each (12). Queens 2 (8). Jacks 1 (4). Total usually around 80 points. Winning the 'Game' point often decides the match.\n\nBidding against the Dealer: The dealer has the last say and can 'steal' the bid for the same amount (in some rules). Bid high enough to force them to 3 or 4.",
